  "final_text": "I created the dashboard and the alert rule in Kibana, and both exist. The rule is switched on and its first check came back with status `ok`. I haven't seen it fire yet, and I couldn't directly test whether its query finds the 5xx requests (details below).\n\n**1. What the data holds.** Each record in `Kibana Sample Data Logs` is one web request, timed by the `timestamp` field. Each one has the response code (`response`, stored as text, e.g. \"200\", \"404\", \"503\"), bytes sent (`bytes`), the visitor's IP address (`clientip`), the page requested (`url`, `request`), file type (`extension`), source and destination country (`geo.src`, `geo.dest`), the visitor's browser (`agent`), operating system (`machine.os`) and the server host (`host`).\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-436c0758 Web logs overview\"** \u2013 7 panels (http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-436c0758-web-logs-overview-1f86e55f157c):\n- Single-number panels: total requests, unique visitors (counted by IP address), total bytes served\n- Requests over time, split by response code (bar chart)\n- Share of each response code (pie chart)\n- Top 10 requested URLs, with request count and average bytes (table)\n- Requests by source country (bar chart)\n\n**3. Alert rule \"EFF-436c0758 5xx spike\"** (id `ceb6888e-76e3-45ab-ae7e-442afbda063f`):\n- It runs every minute against `kibana_sample_data_logs`, using `timestamp` as the time field.\n- It looks for requests whose response code starts with \"5\", i.e. any 5xx code.\n- It fires when more than 10 of them arrive within 5 minutes.\n- **Not tested:** this Kibana blocks queries sent straight to the underlying data, so I couldn't confirm that the \"starts with 5\" match finds the 5xx requests. It relies on the sample data's codes being plain text like \"503\".\n- **No notification set up:** the rule has no connector or action, so when it fires the alert only appears in Kibana. Add a connector (Slack, email, etc.) if you want to be told.",
